 to the ground in discussions of infinite representation and identity.\n\nIn political philosophy and in examinations of the transcendental conditions of thought, Hegel is referenced as shaping the philosophical backdrop to debates on knowledge and time, and to the learning–knowledge distinction. He is depicted as beginning with the essential as a genus and treating the infinite as something that divides the genus and suppresses division in the species, thereby reframing the relationship between form and content. The descriptions also note the infinite movement of evanescence and the ideas of difference and repetition as topics associated with his dialectical method, even as these phrases are linked to later reinterpretations.\n\nA recurring claim is that Hegel embodies the culmination of a long tradition, with his dialectic described as the synthesis of the problem of ideas. In textual contexts, he is often mentioned alongside Kant as a “philosophical labourer” within critique and reason, underscoring his central role in the critique of concepts and the structure of thought. Taken together, the descriptions present Hegel as a German philosopher who forged a comprehensive system—absolute idealism, a robust dialectical method, and a mediation-driven view of history and logic—that illuminates epistemology, metaphysics, and political thought, and that serves as a pivotal reference point in discussions of knowledge, time, and the dynamics of thought.", "WEST:The West is a geographic/civilizational region referenced in the text as a contrast to the Orient in discussions of state form and mobility", "ORIENT:ORIENT is the label used in the text to denote a geographic region. It also functions as a civilizational construct within the same text. The Orient is described in contrast to the West, forming a geographic-civilizational domain that is portrayed as disadvantaged relative to Western powers or frameworks, particularly in the context of state power and nomadism. In summary, ORIENT refers to a geographic region and a civilizational concept that is set in opposition to the West, with perceived disadvantages tied to centralized state power and associations with nomadic practices.", "AMERICA:America is a continent mentioned among the great empires of the Orient, Africa, and America in the passage)<|COMPLETE|>", "EGYPT:Egypt is an ancient civilization cited in the text as the source of the geometrical line that reduces space to the plane. In addition, Egypt is identified as the historical nation referenced as the origin of the Exodus in the context of a war-machine discussion; it is described as the birthplace of the departure of the Israelites in the biblical narrative.", "MOSES:Moses is the biblical figure who applied the nomadic organizational principle to his people during the Exodus; a leading figure associated with leading the Israelites out of Egypt", "JETHRO THE KENITE:Jethro the Kenite is Moses' nomad father-in-law who advised Moses, influencing the narrative around the Exodus", "EXODUS:Exodus is the biblical event describing the departure of Moses and the Israelites from Egypt, central to the discussed war-machine narrative", "FIRST WORLD WAR:A major global conflict cited in the passage to illustrate immobility and speed in warfare and in the context of the war machine.", "SUMER:Ancient civilization in southern Mesopotamia; mentioned in the text as the place of origin and refinement for copper, illustrating early metallurgy provenance", "SIMONDON:SIMONDON (Gilbert Simondon) is the French philosopher discussed in the text, whose ideas are cited as sources for concrete examples about matter, materials, and their transformations—illustrated by materials such as wood, clay, and metals. He is renowned for critiquing the hylomorphic model, challenging the view that form and matter are a fixed, pre-given union. Instead, SIMONDON develops influential concepts, including the machinic phylum, as well as theories of form and matter, and nomadology, in relation to the movement and expression of materiality. His work presents the material world as dynamic and interconnected with technical devices, machines, and their evolution, where form emerges through ongoing processes of transformation. The machinic phylum describes a spectrum or network of machines and technical systems as they relate to living processes, while nomadology emphasizes the non-fixed, mobile trajectories by which materiality expresses itself. Through these ideas, Simondon reframes materiality as active, capable of self-determination within a web of relationships among materials and technologies. Overall, SIMONDON contributes a rigorous philosophical framework for understanding how matter and form co-develop and express themselves in continuous, dynamic processes, highlighting the movement, transformation, and interconnectedness of materials and machines.", "TREATISE ON NOMADOLOGY—THE WAR MACHINE:A philosophical treatise by Simondon on nomadology, the machinic phylum, and the relationship between form and matter.)", "HUSSERL:A philosopher whose ideas are cited in the text as sources for examples about matter, materials, and their transformations (e.g., wood, clay, and metals).", "CLAUSEWITZ:A historical military theorist cited in the text; associated with the debate on total war and the relationship between political aims and military means.", "PROUST:PROUST, specifically Marcel Proust, is a French novelist and essayist best known for In Search of Lost Time. In the referenced passage, Proust’s work and ideas are invoked to illuminate distinctions between the virtual and the real. The text cites “Proust’s formula” for virtuality—real without being actual, ideal without being abstract—and uses it to contrast with Bergson’s critique of the possible. Proust is also positioned as a touchstone for discussions of time and duration; Bergson invokes Proust’s notion of time—such as “a little time in the pure state”—to illustrate duration. Additionally, the descriptions highlight Proust’s role in commentary on states of resonance and his illustrating how love can be oriented toward its own limit or margin, conveying the idea of the ultimate ending within the broader discussion of assemblage. Taken together, PROUST is portrayed as a French writer whose corpus and ideas are employed to clarify philosophical distinctions among virtuality, reality, time, resonance, and the boundaries of love within complex assemblages."], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-large"}}, "key": "embeddings_455b46d4da66ecd8fe82867fbfe9b5c6381dc7827a510d5fe3069575342713b6_v2"}